=== full induct SKILL (source) ===
---
name: induct
description: Conduct one bp3 quest from the main session — read state with bp3 q r, dispatch the one step that state calls for, re-read, until the quest is terminal or a human decision is owed.
---

# Induct

Use when a human in a bp3-enabled repo invokes this skill by
name (`/induct <quest>`, `$induct <quest>`) or asks to "induct",
"run", or "keep going on" a named quest until it lands or
blocks. One quest per induct. One output: step dispatches
through `bp3 q`, ending in an induct report — what reached
`exp_fin`, what is blocked, and what human decision is owed
next. Finish lines have report words: `exp_fin` names an exp
whose state is `landed` (its `land` edge taken); `quest_fin`
names quest promotion to master, the only owner-gated finish
line. The FSM states stay `landed`; `exp_fin`/`quest_fin`
name the finish lines in reports, never new edges.

Use `quest` instead to frame a new quest. Use `using-bp3`
instead for a single state operation by hand (one handoff, one
grade, recovery after a dead session). Use
`red-ink-quest-landing-demo` instead when the quest is done and the
human wants the landing writeup. A design under review is
`red-ink`'s job, not an induct.

## Conductor rule

This skill is the conductor: it owns the loop and nothing
else. It reads state, dispatches the one step the state calls
for, and re-reads. It never does a step's work itself, never
calls a verdict edge for a step, and never re-judges a grade
matrix — it quotes the matrix verbatim. Legality is `bp3 q`'s
call, never this skill's: a refusal is information, answered by
re-reading, not by arguing or retrying unchanged.

Cycles are weather; blocks are decisions. A `needs_fix`
verdict below the cycle cap is weather: the conductor rides
it (Procedure step 3) without paging the owner. `blocked` is
the only page: it means a decision only a human can make is
owed. Fake blocks train rubber-stamps; never manufacture one
out of a cycle.

## No-human variant

With no human present, the `induct-quest-no-human` skill
runs this same conductor over a queue of quests under
no-human rails: preflight before queueing, desks only,
blocked work parks in the inbox instead of paging, a
stated Run budget stops the queue, and the run ends in
the dawn report. No-human never attests (`BP3_DECIDER`
stays unset), never takes `resume` or `delete`, and
never promotes — `quest_fin` stays the owner gate. The
rails live in `skills/induct-quest-no-human/SKILL.md`;
this section names the variant only, so the procedure
above stays the one conductor procedure.

## Procedure

1. Take exactly one argument: the quest. Confirm the engine
   exists: `bp3 q r <quest>` must answer JSON. If `bp3 q` is
   unavailable, stop and say so — never improvise state with
   file edits or another command.
2. Read the world live: `bp3 q r <quest>` gives exp states,
   the railway order, and the blocked inbox. Trust only this
   read — never session memory, `state.yaml`, or a ledger read
   by hand. At a fan-out group, stop and ask the human which
   members run; members are exploration only and never
   landable.
2.5. Quest worktree, by default: before the first dispatch,
   `./bin/bp3 harness desk prepare --quest <quest>` and pass its
   `path` as `desk` in every step's args. All step children
   work in that one worktree for the whole quest; the
   conductor's checkout is never worked in, so parallel
   inducts and the owner's own work cannot collide with the
   Run. If the desk cannot be prepared, stop and say so —
   never fall back to the shared tree. Release it only at
   `quest_fin` or on the owner's word (`bp3 harness desk
   release --quest <quest>`; a dirty desk refuses and is
   preserved).
3. Dispatch exactly one step per read, by state only:

   | State | Dispatch | Args |
   |---|---|---|
   | `todo` | `induct-exp` workflow | `{quest, exp, ts, harness, model, desk}` |
   | `handed_off` | `induct-grade` workflow | `{quest, exp, ts, harness, model, commit, desk}` with `commit` from this read |
   | `approved` | `quest-land` workflow | `{quest, exp, ts, harness, model, desk}` |
   | `blocked` | human gate (step 4) | — |
   | `landed` / `deleted` | none | advance to the next exp, or report at terminal (`landed` is reported as `exp_fin`) |

   `ts` is a UTC compact timestamp (for example
   `20261107T143022Z`) supplied in args because workflow
   scripts cannot read the clock; `harness` and `model` carry
   this session's identity (`muse-code` and its model),
   because workflow children do not inherit the launching
   session's env. Steps are installed with this skill by
   `bp3 skills install`; if a named step is missing, stop and
   say so rather than improvising children. Grade replay is
   free: re-dispatching `induct-grade` on the same `commit`
   is always safe, so a killed grade is simply dispatched
   again — no attempt bookkeeping here.

   Cycle autonomy: when a grade returns `needs_fix` (or a
   verify returns `fail`) and the re-read shows the exp back
   in `todo` below the cycle cap, the conductor dispatches
   the next author cycle itself, carrying the grader's
   named fix — including any regression test or acceptance
   amendment that fix demands — in the next dispatch's
   context. It does not ask the owner about the cycle, does
   not wait for an owner answer, and does not present the
   cycle as a decision. The named fix rides along verbatim;
   the conductor never re-judges it, widens it, or drops it.
   It keeps dispatching grade → author → grade until the exp
   leaves the cycle (`landed`, `blocked`, or `deleted`) or
   the cap is hit. When the re-read shows the cycle cap
   reached (the exp in `blocked` at the cap), the conductor
   stops looping and escalates to the owner through the
   blocked gate instead of dispatching another cycle.
4. Blocked gate (the only page): present the inbox in the
   main session (exp, reason, checkpoint, cycle), capture
   the human's decision in their words, attest it
   (`BP3_DECIDER=human`), and take `resume` or `delete`
   through `bp3 q <quest> u <exp>`. The human never calls bp3
   and never supplies a commit. A blocked Run is never
   resumed in place; after a `resume`, the next read
   dispatches a fresh step. Only human-only calls reach
   this gate: scope changes, `delete`, and moves touching
   another quest's state. The conductor never takes a
   `delete` or a scope change autonomously, at any cycle.
5. Re-read after every dispatch return (`bp3 q r <quest>`)
   and go to step 3. A return that moved nothing (refusal, no
   commit) is re-judged from the fresh read, never retried
   unchanged against a moved tip.
6. Stop when the rollup is terminal (every exp `landed` or
   `deleted`) or a human decision is owed that this session
   cannot get. Name each `landed` exp `exp_fin` in the
   report; when the quest is promoted to master, name that
   finish line `quest_fin` (owner gate, demo first). Report: dispatches per exp with their ledger
   evidence, the grade matrices quoted verbatim, Runs and
   tapes left behind, scorecard fields (wall time, context
   tokens, human-active minutes — or "unmeasured", never
   zero), and the next decision owed.

## Rules

- The skill writes no state. `bp3 q u` is the only writer,
  called by step children (and at the blocked gate, by this
  session on attested human words); ledger files, `state.yaml`,
  and branch tips are never hand-edited, and tape is evidence,
  never parsed into facts.
- Human attestation comes only from actual human words in
  this session. The engine never attests on the human's
  behalf, and `resume` without it is refused
  (`E_HUMAN_REQUIRED`) — that refusal is the gate working.
  Attestation is owed at the blocked gate only; a
  `needs_fix` cycle below the cap needs no attestation,
  because no human decision is being recorded.
- Identity rides env (`BP3_HARNESS`, `BP3_MODEL`) set per Run
  by the launcher or hooks. Unset means `unknown` plus a
  warning in the verdict; never guess a model name.
- Verdicts bind the exact commit reviewed. A verdict naming a
  superseded commit is refused; dispatch a fresh step against
  the current commit instead of arguing with the refusal.
- Instructions only: this skill holds no state, spawns no
  daemon, and assumes no runtime beyond the main session, its
  workflow children, and the `bp3 q` binary.

=== induct-quest ===
---
name: induct-quest
description: Alias for induct — conduct one bp3 quest from the main session with exactly the induct procedure; this body is a pointer, never a fork.
---

# Induct quest

`/induct-quest <quest>` means exactly `/induct <quest>`.

Follow the canonical `induct` skill (`skills/induct/SKILL.md`)
procedure and rules as written there — read state with
`bp3 q r`, dispatch the one step that state calls for,
re-read. This file carries no procedure of its own, so the
two names cannot drift.

=== induct-quest-no-human full ===
---
name: induct-quest-no-human
description: Run several bp3 quests with no human present using the induct conductor — fleet preflight, park blocked work in the inbox, and end with the check-in report; never attests, never pages, never promotes.
---

# Induct quest, no human

Use when a human in a bp3-enabled repo invokes this skill
by name (`/induct-quest-no-human <quest>...`,
`$induct-quest-no-human`) or asks to run quests unattended,
overnight, or in a queue with no human present. Several
quests may be named; a Run budget may be stated. One
output: the check-in report (step 5). For one quest with a
human present, use `induct` instead.

`/induct-quest-no-human <quest>...` means `/induct <quest>` per
quest, in queue order, under the no-human rails below. Follow
the canonical `induct` skill (`skills/induct/SKILL.md`)
procedure and rules as written there — read state with
`bp3 q r`, dispatch the one step that state calls for,
re-read. This file carries no step procedure of its own,
so the two names cannot drift; it adds only the rails
that make the conductor safe with no human in the room.

## Shape

This ships as a skill mode of `induct`, not its own FSM.
The rails below (preflight, parking, budget, check-in report)
all state as conductor rules over the existing exp FSM
without special cases: the queue is ordered iteration,
parking is stopping a quest on a `blocked` read, the
budget is a stop on dispatching, and the check-in report is
a read. No queue-level state is written anywhere; queue
words name report outcomes, never new edges.

## Fleet

Several named quests are one fleet, not several queues.
A fleet declares its merge order before the queue starts:
the order quests were named, unless the invoker names a
different order in the same breath. The merge order is
fixed for the whole fleet; finishing first never moves a
quest up it. Names below reuse the exp's words: `fleet`,
`footprint`, `merge order`, and `check-in report`.

- Footprint: the paths a quest declares it will touch —
  every path named in its QUEST.md Scope and in each
  exp's Output. Two footprints overlap when they name
  the same path, or one names a directory the other
  names a path under. A quest with no declared footprint
  fails preflight: disjointness cannot be shown for it.
- Disjoint quests run in parallel: each quest rides its
  own conductor loop at the same time, desks per child,
  one quest per checkout. A parked quest never holds up
  a disjoint quest; parking stops only its own quest.
- The fleet never merges: quests ready for `quest_fin`
  are only listed, in merge order, for the owner to
  promote one at a time in that order.

## No-human rails

- Desks only: every step Run works from a prepared
  `bp3 harness desk` desk, one quest per checkout. No-human
  never works in the shared tree and never runs two
  quests in one checkout at once.
- No-human never attests: `BP3_DECIDER` stays unset for
  the whole run, exactly as the launcher left it.
  No-human never takes `resume` or `delete`, and never
  pages a question to the owner. A decision only a human
  can make is not asked for; the work parks (below) and
  waits for morning.
- No-human never promotes or pushes: `quest_fin` stays the
  owner gate. A quest whose exps all reach `exp_fin`
  stops there; promotion waits for the owner.

## Procedure

1. Preflight each named quest before queueing it, and
   declare the merge order before the queue starts. A
   quest is not queued — and is reported with the failed
   check named — when any of these fails:
   - every exp's EXPERIMENT.md carries Acceptance rows
     (an exp missing Acceptance rows fails this check);
   - no EXPERIMENT.md in the quest carries an open
     design question;
   - `make check` is green (`make check` red fails
     this check);
   - the quest declares a footprint (see Fleet).
   Footprint preflight: in merge order, the first quest
   claims its footprint; a quest whose footprint overlaps
   an already-admitted quest's is refused at preflight.
   An overlapping quest is refused at preflight, named
   in the report, with zero Runs spent on it. Anything
   shared queues serially in a later fleet, never in
   parallel in this one.
   A quest that fails preflight is reported, not queued;
   the queue moves on to the next named quest.
2. Run admitted quests in parallel, each in merge order
   for reporting. Per quest, ride the `induct` conductor
   loop: `needs_fix` below the cycle cap rides to the
   next cycle (Exp 6 rule, no owner question); `landed`
   is reported as `exp_fin` and the conductor advances
   to the next exp.
3. Park, never page: when an exp enters `blocked`, or
   the cycle cap is hit, park it — the exp stays in the
   blocked inbox with its reason, its quest stops, and
   the fleet moves on to the other quests. Nothing is
   resumed, deleted, or attested to unpark it.
4. Fleet budget: one budget caps the whole fleet, not
   one budget per quest. Every step Run in every quest
   counts against the one fleet budget. When the fleet
   budget is spent, every quest stops, even mid-quest.
   No new step is dispatched in any quest after the
   budget is spent, and the report says the budget stopped it.
5. Check-in report (the only output): one combined
   report for the whole fleet, from `bp3 q r`, the run
   registry, and `bp3 harness desk list` — never from
   memory or tape text:
   - quests ready for `quest_fin`, listed in the
     predeclared merge order (fleet never merges);
   - exps at `exp_fin` (state `landed`), per quest;
   - parked items from all quests in one check-in list,
     grouped by quest, each with its inbox reason;
   - shared-decision parks: parked items whose inbox
     reasons name the same human decision appear as one
     decision with its dependent quests listed; owner
     words are still attested per exp at resume time,
     by a human, outside the fleet;
   - refusals from preflight, each quest named with the
     failed check or the footprint it overlapped;
   - `usage_recorded` token totals summed per quest;
   - the worktree sweep result: `bp3 harness desk list`
     output, naming any desk still open.
   Then stop. Judgment waits for a human; this report
   is where the night hands over.

## Rules

- The skill writes no state. `bp3 q u` is the only
  writer, called by step children; no-human itself takes
  no verdict edge and no human edge.
- Identity rides env (`BP3_HARNESS`, `BP3_MODEL`) as in
  `induct`; `BP3_DECIDER` is the one env name no-human
  must never set.
- Instructions only: this skill holds no state, spawns
  no daemon, and assumes no runtime beyond the main
  session, its workflow children, and the `bp3 q`
  binary.
